"""Whole-exchange source-reviewed choices for Maria and the Sao Jorge bandits."""
REVISIONS={
5:('Whisper, whisper...',"Preserve the repeated quiet-whisper sound as an impersonal cue. The repetition opens the overheard exchange;it is not an imperative to whisper,a summons or an invented named speaker's words."),
13:("This is bad. There's no way we can make a move...","Keep the already natural opening assessment and restore the practical inability to act. 'Touch that now' sounds translated and may suggest handling an object. The vague obstacle remains vague;no force size,battle or guard arrangement is invented."),
16:("...Are you the bandits who've been attacking Sao Jorge?","Restore Maria's hesitant lead-in and direct identification question. The past attacks remain directed at the source-named town with the approved Sao Jorge spelling. This does not establish that the present encounter is in the town or invent a particular raid's casualties."),
19:("W-what?! Who the hell are you?","Restore the startled stammer and rough hostility of the bandit's demand. The colloquial intensifier conveys てめえ without inventing a personal relationship or status. It contrasts with Maria's controlled introduction."),
22:("I'm with {MACRO:FO}.","Make the affiliation a natural complete reply to 'Who are you?' while preserving the exact source faction substitution. The currently measured default is Li Clan;the prose does not hardcode it or claim leadership,rank or town ownership."),
30:("Thanks to you, we can't raid the town! You've put us out of business!","Localize メシの食い上げ as losing one's livelihood,which is the bandit's self-interested complaint. Remove the unsupported prediction 'We will starve.' The colloquial business idiom retains the irony of treating raiding as work without endorsing it as narration."),
33:("Bold words for thieves! We can't abandon the townspeople when they need our help!","Retain the effective stern rebuke and restore Xien's refusal to leave distressed residents without help. 'That town needs our help' alone loses the obligation that answers the bandit's grievance. No military rank,kinship or command over Maria is added."),
40:("Say what you want! We'll have our revenge!","Restore the defiance of anyone's objections and the emphatic revenge vow. The contraction suits the bandit's rough voice. No specific target,method or promised death is supplied."),
43:("Wait. The ones to blame are the corrupt slave traders here in Africa and the brutal soldiers who plunder it.","Preserve Maria's interruption and targeted attribution of blame to corrupt merchants trading slaves in Africa and brutal plundering soldiers. 'Slave traders' naturally combines the source merchant/activity phrase. The restrictive 'the ones to blame' expresses her argument;it is character speech,not narrator-provided historical absolution. Do not omit or soften the atrocities."),
46:("If you hate every European and attack people indiscriminately, you're no better than those brutes. You're just as guilty.","Restore the explicit hatred of all Europeans and the concluding equal-guilt judgment. The earlier version loses both and makes the condemnation more generic. Maria challenges indiscriminate attacks in response to the stated grievance;the moral claim remains her speech."),
49:("You talk like you know it all!","Restore the idiomatic rebuke 聞いた風なことを:the bandit rejects Maria's apparent all-knowing stance. 'Easy to say' changes the objection. The natural hostile reply supplies the resistance before she argues that revenge will not restore happiness."),
53:("Give up on revenge. It's pointless. It won't make you happy again, will it?","Restore the pointlessness statement and rhetorical question while expressing the recovery-of-happiness idea in natural English. Maria remains firm rather than offering compensation or a promise to undo particular losses. The reply develops the preceding moral challenge."),
57:("It'll only start the fighting all over again. More of your people will suffer.","Preserve recurrence of conflict and the further suffering of the bandit's own companions. 'All over again' is a natural rendering of the repeated cycle. No specific future casualty,death or retaliation is invented."),
61:("...Th-then what are we supposed to do?!","Restore the source hesitation as anger gives way to an uncertain demand for an alternative. 'Supposed to do' naturally expresses asking Maria what course she expects. It is not a submissive order request or completed change of allegiance."),
64:("We have to end the European nations' struggle for power. It's causing so much fighting across the world's seas.","Preserve the proposed end to competing European states' struggle for power and its connection to many maritime conflicts worldwide. Two natural sentences replace the predecessor's awkward four-row fragment structure. This remains an ambitious goal,not a claim of achievement or a new political doctrine."),
72:("The great powers are about to carve up the whole world. They're seeking the Proofs of Conquest to justify it. We're gathering them to stop that.","Restore impending partition of the whole world and the missing reason the powers seek the Proofs:as a pretext to justify that partition. Reorder the last two clauses for clear English reference while keeping Maria's preventive purpose. Use current Help4 and reviewed Help189's Proof of Conquest term;earlier exact-record Supremacy/Conquest variation remains separately open. No powers,ownership rules or numerical thresholds for the objects are invented."),
75:("Stop the great powers?! A-are you out of your mind?","Restore the incredulous stammer and localize the sanity challenge as a natural rough question. The bandit doubts the scale of Maria's proposed goal;he does not concede a victory or question a newly invented rank."),
78:("I'm not bluffing. We've extended our influence all the way to Africa. We're confident we can face them on at least equal terms.","Preserve the denial of bluffing,the expansion of influence as far as Africa,and confidence in dealing on at least equal terms. Restore 'at least';the source allows equality or better. This is Maria's confident assessment,not proof of fleet strength,a victory or merely having traveled to Africa."),
85:("Well? How about leaving that revenge of yours in our hands?","Make the source invitation a voluntary question rather than the predecessor's command. Keep the revenge-entrusting image as the rhetorical pivot after rejecting indiscriminate violence. This offers another course through their venture;it promises no specific killing or retaliatory act and leaves the bandits' agency intact."),
92:("Come sail with us. With that kind of spirit, I know you've got what it takes.","Use a natural invitation and encouraging idiom for the source's confidence in the bandits' grit. 'You've got what it takes' leaves the specific task as contextual as the Japanese. No rank,salary,oath,kinship or earned recruitment state is invented."),
103:("All right. We'll take a chance on you!","Restore the voluntary gamble/trust idiom 賭けてみよう instead of replacing it with unqualified existing trust. The decision follows a silence and the admission that staying will accomplish nothing. The bandit remains hopeful and rough-spoken."),
114:("I don't know if this has anything to do with that Proof you're looking for, but it was hidden in the ruins.","Preserve the uncertainty about a connection to the sought Proof and the fact that the object was hidden in ruins. 'Has anything to do with' is natural American speech. No finder,location name,ruin culture,completed Proof or earned inventory result is invented. The native catalogue's Upper Stone Tablet label does not authorize inserting that full title here."),
117:('A stone tablet...?',"Restore the source material and hesitant questioning identification. 'Tablet?' alone drops stone and the reflective pause. This remains Maria's tentative reaction,not confirmation that the item is a complete Proof.")
}
RETAIN_NOTES={
9:"Retain the wordless question as Maria's brief puzzled response to overheard whispers. ASCII question punctuation represents the existing full-width presentation form without adding a spoken question or inferred suspicion.",
26:"Retain 'What?! You helped guard that town! You ruined everything for us!' as natural,angry hyperbole that carries the startled recognition,security-assistance accusation and strong inconvenience complaint. The following livelihood line explains the problem. It neither assigns a formal military appointment nor states narrator-confirmed total ruin.",
37:"Retain 'Shut up! Europeans destroyed our village!' as blunt,fluent speech matching the bandit's rage and the source's devastation. 'Destroyed' preserves the seriousness of メチャクチャ without inventing burning,deaths or the enslavement of specific villagers. The broad blame is his allegation,which Maria challenges next.",
68:"Retain the surprised !? reaction to Maria's proposal to stop the European struggle for power. Normalizing its full-width punctuation adds no words or invented action;the abrupt interruption is part of the source progression.",
81:"Retain the ellipsis as a source-authored silent response after Maria asserts confidence. It lets the claim sink in without inventing spoken agreement or a physical gesture. The source speech command and selector remain complete.",
88:"Retain 'What?!' as a natural startled response to the invitation to entrust revenge. The surprise is distinct from the earlier hostile demand and precedes Maria's explanation;no extra doubt or acceptance is needed.",
96:"Retain the second ellipsis as consideration after the invitation and encouragement. It precedes the bandit's own assessment and voluntary decision. Adding acceptance here would flatten that progression.",
100:"Retain 'Staying here changes nothing...' as natural,resigned acknowledgment that remaining in the present situation offers no way forward. It bridges the reflective silence and the decision to take a chance on Maria without inventing an evacuation order or a destroyed present location.",
107:"Retain 'Welcome.' as Maria's brief,controlled and warm answer to the voluntary acceptance. The source is equally compact;adding a position,rank or promise would introduce new facts. The following original effect and item exchange stay separate."
}
